the people who run zimbabwe  are sending its citizens a powerful  message  descent in any form will not be  tolerated  here residents of a township in the  capital harare  were beaten for sitting outside  the government of emerson managua has  shut the country down  after opposition groups called for  protests against corruption  and economic mismanagement but the  president saw it differently  accusing critics of insurrection  however a small number did take to the  streets and dusty tracks  and there were some who were brave  enough to show their faces  like award-winning author sitsi  dungarenga  she was bundled into a police truck by  armed officers  shortly after this photo was taken  our team caught up with the writer who's  been nominated for this year's booker  prize  after she was released on bail and she  told us  she has the constitutional right to  fight for change  i feel that probably all zimbabweans  want a better life for all zimbabweans  and the people who live here  and i think that that's a very right  motive to have it's a good thing to live  by  and to work for  dozens of government critics have been  arrested including the spokesperson of  the main opposition party  the mdc right police have arrived  broadcast her own arrest before being  charged with  inciting public violence and breaching  the rules on  covet 19. it's an indictment on who  we've become as a society in zimbabwe  that people cannot step outside their  homes  and protest um against all the  injustices that we see  the fact that corruption is killing us  the fact that we've got a hunger crisis  the fact that our public health system  is on its knees  if we're criminalizing the very fact of  speaking out  that cannot be the zimbabwe that we're  after it means we've got a very long way  to go  emerson manning took power in 2017  promising economic growth and  world-class public services  but he's fast becoming associated with  the worst practices of his predecessor  the long-time dictator robert mugabe  is all over if you could just throw this  up  a pattern of arbitrary arrests and  abductions  is now well established last year we  interviewed union leader albert  massouera  who said he was kidnapped and beaten by  agents of the state  when he called for an increase in  teachers pay  it seems acts of protest have been  officially banned  by a paranoid government determined to  suppress  dissent journalist hopewell chinono  was arrested after revealing kickbacks  in the health department  but his voice and those of many others  will not be silenced  john sparks sky news
 

